Prevalence and the normalization mechanism, configuration ariel, pipeline norm_xgb

1. Precision at realistic base rates. The grid is balanced by construction, so every
   accuracy in the paper assumes a 50 % prevalence. Recall and specificity are properties
   of the classifier and do not change with prevalence; precision does.

   threshold  recall  specif.    π=0.5    π=0.2    π=0.1   π=0.05   π=0.01
       0.500   89.8%    90.9%    90.8%    71.1%    52.2%    34.1%     9.0%
       0.215   95.0%    83.7%    85.3%    59.3%    39.3%    23.5%     5.6%
       0.900   78.4%    98.1%    97.6%    91.1%    81.9%    68.2%    29.2%
       0.990   66.8%    99.8%    99.6%    98.6%    96.8%    93.5%    73.5%

   at π = 0.10, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.444 and retains 90.6 % recall
   at π = 0.05, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.793 and retains 82.8 % recall
   at π = 0.01, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.960 and retains 73.8 % recall

2. Why per-spectrum normalization helps: the absolute-depth nuisance direction.
   Raw vs normalized features trained and tested on radius-restricted populations,
   training-set size held fixed at 4,000 planets per band.

    radius (R_E)      raw   normalized      gap   depth span
            1-26   63.05%       89.12%  +26.07      4.40 dex
             1-6   57.93%       88.92%  +30.98      4.35 dex
            6-12   64.06%       88.84%  +24.77      2.91 dex
           12-20   70.53%       89.86%  +19.33      2.31 dex

   The normalization advantage falls from +26.1 points on the full grid to
   +19.3 on the narrowest band, tracking the shrinking spread of absolute transit depth.
